[[:db/retract 17592186198874 :question/answer-fields 17592186197479] {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" :answer/id #uuid "fa75c1a2-6d08-5d0a-83da-4e5fb19c78b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/ec213c0f6ddca4b1f1eecc4708609f0e7d49660e64c66df1f0010f88433c9f20" :answer/id #uuid "d7a64d53-7547-501b-a8af-b695293dca5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/77fbe30dc0f74eab7e53c43bcdf19c3f31d206df1eb5df1422235fdc0d01a6d2" :answer/id #uuid "f1df5ad1-61cd-5a64-9baf-0380d4e0be3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" :answer/id #uuid "7337adf4-fa31-5a0e-8c72-8bed3669df80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/1eb1804da18743b97431502562dd001769c020cc36cc99120254af18d7266d4c" :answer/id #uuid "2daf20f9-35d3-50a1-afa1-e689113776ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe44d904-22dc-581f-bf87-b0620523ea0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/77fbe30dc0f74eab7e53c43bcdf19c3f31d206df1eb5df1422235fdc0d01a6d2" :answer-field/choices ["q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/b532b1f0190951620c43b2b96537c1278e6e0c9e02b763e37b5435ef496bab1d" "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/ec213c0f6ddca4b1f1eecc4708609f0e7d49660e64c66df1f0010f88433c9f20" "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/77fbe30dc0f74eab7e53c43bcdf19c3f31d206df1eb5df1422235fdc0d01a6d2" "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/37a56622655a103e2d770d7f8bd9249738b40ca9ae8a13d89445e535cff51e86" "q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944/1eb1804da18743b97431502562dd001769c020cc36cc99120254af18d7266d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38079"] :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38079/selection/ma-v1/86bf0e7be0451be69915f12f8bf5aa9925d1f0c3b733156dd08dced4617b9944"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection/bec3f5631aa3b5b1f8876d1263132d597cba431ecfe01fe4a15f343e70adb298" :answer/id #uuid "117a3787-4689-50b2-8d5e-b089cef5ea75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection/2efbd31c9b8a4e1de3259c68fcef8e9c48ec8b437bf8dee82ce81ebc927dd606" :answer/id #uuid "5ccadb85-e113-51b9-bce1-daf966881d91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection/5f9c095a730e36cd16a55a27c764f4ab3b6586403207e48e222e58770fae958d" :answer/id #uuid "ef1060b0-1c79-5ac4-ae60-79e90d2cf792" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection/e1b532818463229bb500608a3e4285418160fd2535d0b46e02b49e216c8d64d7" :answer/id #uuid "7e0547ca-273f-5986-8360-90634311b13b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection/4b85c8da3d1d8155e1df499739ff8a3382032635d2ff1e07a9ac753863a98b4b" :answer/id #uuid "af3257b3-522c-5156-ad27-7ec5fe374536" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "14a83e38-7775-590f-a49f-70dd043e72f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38245/selection/5f9c095a730e36cd16a55a27c764f4ab3b6586403207e48e222e58770fae958d" :answer-field/choices ["q-38245/selection/bec3f5631aa3b5b1f8876d1263132d597cba431ecfe01fe4a15f343e70adb298" "q-38245/selection/2efbd31c9b8a4e1de3259c68fcef8e9c48ec8b437bf8dee82ce81ebc927dd606" "q-38245/selection/5f9c095a730e36cd16a55a27c764f4ab3b6586403207e48e222e58770fae958d" "q-38245/selection/e1b532818463229bb500608a3e4285418160fd2535d0b46e02b49e216c8d64d7" "q-38245/selection/4b85c8da3d1d8155e1df499739ff8a3382032635d2ff1e07a9ac753863a98b4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38245" :question/id #uuid "48feab89-768b-5b8e-8c0a-02f86fdff070" :question/math-academy-id "q-38245" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38245/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38245"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-81319"] :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection/994ba792582e9f0cd98abb47e2eb2e62fee06c608b6f1b9ad3d62ee92e09851a" :answer/id #uuid "f6e5e006-1b27-547f-89f1-e0bf72d6c425" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection/15290b35d7cf57abc907c60e8c16bb92ce90e85a601831d2e5c594eb04bf2433" :answer/id #uuid "d2433591-06e6-5786-ae94-916b90ab9a2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection/e1b532818463229bb500608a3e4285418160fd2535d0b46e02b49e216c8d64d7" :answer/id #uuid "473e9830-80c3-5dcf-89d4-ac135579a3e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection/bec3f5631aa3b5b1f8876d1263132d597cba431ecfe01fe4a15f343e70adb298" :answer/id #uuid "b6aa4e1c-d9ca-5cb6-a1f0-9dcc0dc868d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection/8059caa1952472cbe72148f0c089a91bf45f670f7d986d9372c6281cf325ea05" :answer/id #uuid "f6bc6899-080a-5546-a994-4f50159fd979" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0aeab8-1cb7-5313-abf6-d718134ac07e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160465/selection/e1b532818463229bb500608a3e4285418160fd2535d0b46e02b49e216c8d64d7" :answer-field/choices ["q-160465/selection/994ba792582e9f0cd98abb47e2eb2e62fee06c608b6f1b9ad3d62ee92e09851a" "q-160465/selection/15290b35d7cf57abc907c60e8c16bb92ce90e85a601831d2e5c594eb04bf2433" "q-160465/selection/e1b532818463229bb500608a3e4285418160fd2535d0b46e02b49e216c8d64d7" "q-160465/selection/bec3f5631aa3b5b1f8876d1263132d597cba431ecfe01fe4a15f343e70adb298" "q-160465/selection/8059caa1952472cbe72148f0c089a91bf45f670f7d986d9372c6281cf325ea05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160465" :question/id #uuid "6940edb0-4ff9-5079-afa0-d099bcae85dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160465" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160465/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160465"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection/821dd8ed7daef7398474de64a02ed06acb0ff0876b3c43c33c7514bed5d0390b" :answer/id #uuid "aa7b7217-a4de-5746-9577-1dcc7d0e28c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection/a4b6ec45e2f2d0419d9fff4723df066124aa53d40e92cc78c3806595b04c2bc5" :answer/id #uuid "6dfe7165-2b6c-565e-94c2-374be8c74afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection/ae77727946e13ff8d43530e7f2a41e554db0608515a50f898fa991cac9a12925" :answer/id #uuid "eba09d48-b412-5438-b1c7-97c3c501170a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection/20a745679939027c17eef4c3895bf2ffbc2a1fb2373021f8577c49a7a5302b1e" :answer/id #uuid "bba65baa-7359-5bd4-b666-c85b5debabca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection/7da221f38962f531bca199a8c74eec9b167e92aad10e448a1c16d49e2a85cbd3" :answer/id #uuid "704abbbd-e91c-51c1-bed8-4e24c6889c90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b1f2e3-10b8-56e9-909f-37dcc42ae1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160582/selection/20a745679939027c17eef4c3895bf2ffbc2a1fb2373021f8577c49a7a5302b1e" :answer-field/choices ["q-160582/selection/821dd8ed7daef7398474de64a02ed06acb0ff0876b3c43c33c7514bed5d0390b" "q-160582/selection/a4b6ec45e2f2d0419d9fff4723df066124aa53d40e92cc78c3806595b04c2bc5" "q-160582/selection/ae77727946e13ff8d43530e7f2a41e554db0608515a50f898fa991cac9a12925" "q-160582/selection/20a745679939027c17eef4c3895bf2ffbc2a1fb2373021f8577c49a7a5302b1e" "q-160582/selection/7da221f38962f531bca199a8c74eec9b167e92aad10e448a1c16d49e2a85cbd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160582" :question/id #uuid "f5c689ee-f9f4-5966-afaa-66a344e58af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160582" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-160582/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160582"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
